{"question_id": "54026fce", "question_type": "single-session-preference", "response": "Based on what's worked in your thinking so far — you already like the virtual coffee break idea and plan to raise it at your next team meeting — here are some ways to build on that:\n\n- **Run with the virtual coffee breaks.** Pick a reliable, easy-to-use video platform, set a regular schedule, and frame it as a collaborative suggestion rather than a mandate. Your proposed ground rules (no work talk, keep it light and casual) are a good anchor — you could add: respect everyone's time, be present, use video, and rotate conversation starters.\n- **Add occasional team-building activities.** Virtual games, trivia, or online escape rooms can break up the routine and give people something to bond over beyond small talk.\n- **Keep connection going between calls.** An informal Slack or Teams channel for non-work chatter (pets, shows, weekend plans) recreates the watercooler feeling asynchronously — this directly addresses the social interaction you've been missing.\n- **Gauge interest first.** A quick team discussion or short survey before launching anything will help you tailor the format and timing to what colleagues actually want.\n\nSince you're planning to bring this up at the next team meeting, the coffee break idea plus a casual chat channel would be the lowest-effort, highest-impact combination to start with.", "n_records": 12, "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 2280, "completion_tokens": 1023, "calls": 2}, "selected": ["self|work_from_home_preference", "self|remote_work_social_isolation", "self|virtual_coffee_break_interest", "self|team_meeting_plan", "self|virtual_coffee_break_ground_rules", "self|virtual_coffee_break_schedule", "self|video_conferencing_preference", "assistant|virtual_coffee_break_suggestion", "assistant|online_team_activities_suggestion", "assistant|social_channels_suggestion", "assistant|discuss_and_agree_suggestion", "assistant|coffee_break_ground_rules_suggestion"], "digest_bytes": 2129}
{"question_id": "09d032c9", "question_type": "single-session-preference", "response": "Memory only records that you own a portable power bank and a wireless charging pad, along with several power bank options and their specs/prices — it does not contain phone battery-life tips (screen settings, app management, etc.).\n\nWhat memory does support: since you already carry a power bank, upgrading to a higher-capacity one would more than double your on-the-go charge. The 10,000mAh options each charge a phone up to 3 times — Jackery Titan S ($25, multiple USB ports), Aukey 10,000mAh ($20), Eggtronic 10,000mAh ($30, with USB-C), and Anker PowerCore Fusion ($35, with USB-C) — versus 1.5–2 charges from 5,000mAh models like the Anker PowerCore 5000 ($15) or Mophie Powerstation Mini ($30).", "n_records": 49, "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 5283, "completion_tokens": 870, "calls": 2}, "selected": ["self|portable_power_bank", "self|wireless_charging_pad", "self|apple_watch", "self|travel_habits", "self|tv_purchase_plan", "assistant|tech_organization_suggestion", "assistant|travel_case_option", "assistant|bellroy_tech_kit_pros", "assistant|bellroy_tech_kit_cons", "assistant|peak_design_tech_pouch_pros", "assistant|peak_design_tech_pouch_cons", "assistant|cable_organizer_option", "assistant|power_bank_option", "assistant|lg_tv_lineup", "assistant|lg_tv_deal", "assistant|lg_tv_recommendation", "assistant|lg_oled_c9_picture_quality", "assistant|lg_oled_c9_viewing_angles", "assistant|lg_oled_c9_hdr", "assistant|lg_oled_c9_processor", "assistant|lg_oled_c9_gaming", "assistant|lg_oled_c9_ai_image_enhancement", "assistant|lg_oled_c9_audio"], "digest_bytes": 6303}
{"question_id": "95228167", "question_type": "single-session-preference", "response": "Based on your profile, here's what to focus on during your store visit:\n\n**Try before you buy.** You were previously advised to play both the Stratocaster and Les Paul to see which one feels and sounds like \"home\" — that's still the top priority @2023-05-21.\n\n**Look for coil-tap/coil-split functionality.** Since you want extra tonal flexibility (especially for open D), prioritize a Les Paul with this feature. Models to look for:\n- **Les Paul Standard** — coil-tap on the tone controls\n- **Les Paul Traditional** — coil-splitting\n- **Les Paul Custom** — often includes coil-tap or coil-split @2023-05-21\n\n**Test it for your actual playing styles.**\n- For **open D tuning**: try the neck pickup for a warmer, mellower tone, and check that the low register plays smoothly @2023-05-21\n- For **acoustic-like fingerpicking** (e.g., the \"Blackbird\" intro): use a lighter touch and the neck pickup — note that the Les Paul is generally better suited to rock, blues, and heavy genres than delicate fingerpicking, so see if it still delivers the sound you want @2023-05-21\n\n**Practical checks:** Since you'll likely use heavier strings for open D (0.012–0.052 or 0.013–0.056), test how the guitar feels under higher string tension, and factor in that you'll want the action and intonation set up for low-register playing @2023-05-21.", "n_records": 13, "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 2690, "completion_tokens": 1060, "calls": 2}, "selected": ["self|current_guitar", "self|guitar_interest_open_d_tuning", "self|playing_interest_fingerpicking", "self|guitar_feature_preference", "assistant|guitar_purchase_advice", "assistant|open_d_setup_strings", "assistant|open_d_setup_guitar", "assistant|open_d_amp_settings", "assistant|open_d_pickup_advice", "assistant|open_d_technique_advice", "assistant|les_paul_coil_tap_models", "assistant|les_paul_fingerpicking_tips", "assistant|les_paul_genre_fit"], "digest_bytes": 2480}
